! Bismuth, 20 atoms with spin-orbit coupling -- bi10-soc.in doubled along a1,
! for the GPU size sweep. Fully-relativistic ultrasoft dataset, noncollinear.
! **Ten sites with spin-orbit coupling.** Bismuthene -- the buckled-free flat
! honeycomb monolayer of `bismuthene-soc-small.in` -- in a cell five times as
! long along `a1`, so ten bismuth atoms, two-component spinors and the
! `j`-resolved projectors of a fully-relativistic ultrasoft dataset.
!
! The cell is written with `CELL_PARAMETERS` rather than `ibrav = 4` because it
! is no longer hexagonal: stretching one of the two in-plane vectors by five
! leaves a monoclinic lattice whose point group is what the two codes have to
! agree on. That is the same question `si10-nc.in` asks, in the regime where a
! wrong answer also rotates spinors.
!
! **One k-point**, where the two-atom cell uses 6x6x1. That is a budget and it
! is stated rather than hidden. A fully-relativistic `dn` dataset carries
! fifteen valence electrons per atom, so ten atoms is 150 electrons and --
! being noncollinear -- 150 *spinor* bands, on the 216 x 45 x 81 grid that
! stretching an already-large 2D cell by five forces. `pw.x` spends 200 seconds
! on one SCF iteration here with four k-points. What is being compared is two
! codes on one sampling of one geometry, and every path spin-orbit coupling
! adds -- the `j`-resolved projectors, `fcoef`, the 2x2 `D_ij` in spin space,
! the spinor density and its symmetrisation -- is exercised at one k-point
! exactly as at forty.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 0, celldm(1) = 10.109950,
    nat = 20, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 20.0, ecutrho = 160.0,
    noncolin = .true., lspinorb = .true.,
    starting_magnetization = 0.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.01,
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
CELL_PARAMETERS alat
  10.000000000000  0.000000000000  0.000000000000
  -0.500000000000  0.866025403784  0.000000000000
   0.000000000000  0.000000000000  1.978248000000
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Bi 208.98 Bi.rel-pbe-dn-rrkjus_psl.1.0.0.UPF
